Part 1.

24
00:02:02.126 --> 00:02:04.046
Introduction and Why It Matters.

25
00:02:05.296 --> 00:02:08.517
A story: the outage that lasted longer than its cause.

26
00:02:08.797 --> 00:02:15.005
At 7:02 pm on a Saturday, inventory-service's database failed over (Ch 10).

27
00:02:15.285 --> 00:02:17.461
The failover took 25 seconds.

28
00:02:17.741 --> 00:02:21.112
The checkout outage that followed lasted 41 minutes.

29
00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:26.661
Every checkout pod retried inventory calls immediately, three times, at every layer.

30
00:02:26.941 --> 00:02:34.557
When the new primary came up, it received roughly 30 times its normal load in the first second, and its connection pool saturated.

31
00:02:34.837 --> 00:02:38.933
Many requests now timed out, and those timeouts triggered more retries.

32
00:02:39.213 --> 00:02:42.605
The database was up, but the system couldn't get back to working.

33
00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.281
Inventory's request queue was unbounded.

34
00:02:46.561 --> 00:02:52.491
Requests waited 8 to 20 seconds in the queue, long after the app (3 s budget) had given up.

35
00:02:52.771 --> 00:03:01.881
Inventory spent almost all its capacity finishing work nobody was waiting for, so goodput stayed near zero while CPU showed 100 %.

36
00:03:02.161 --> 00:03:11.377
No circuit breaker: every checkout thread kept calling inventory and waiting for the timeout, so checkout itself ran out of threads (Ch 17).

37
00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:20.745
No fallback: browsing the catalog also called inventory (to show only 3 left), so browsing failed too, although it didn't need live stock.

38
00:03:22.095 --> 00:03:28.367
The fix that worked, at 7:43 pm: someone scaled checkout down to zero for two minutes.

39
00:03:28.647 --> 00:03:32.700
The load vanished, inventory drained its queue, and everything recovered.

40
00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.057
The system was metastable: the trigger was over, but the overload sustained itself.

41
00:03:38.337 --> 00:03:41.303
This chapter's patterns are exactly what was missing:

42
00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:45.956
Retries with exponential backoff and jitter, at one layer, with a budget.

43
00:03:46.236 --> 00:03:54.641
Load shedding: bounded queues, deadline-aware dropping and fast 503s, so capacity goes to requests that can still succeed.

44
00:03:55.991 --> 00:04:00.770
Circuit breakers, so callers stop hammering a failing dependency and fail fast.

45
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.527
Bulkheads, so one dependency can't consume every thread.

46
00:04:04.807 --> 00:04:10.802
Fallbacks and graceful degradation: show in stock from cache instead of failing the catalog page.

47
00:04:11.082 --> 00:04:14.922
Rate limits to protect shared dependencies from any single caller.

48
00:04:16.272 --> 00:04:16.955
Quote:

49
00:04:17.235 --> 00:04:18.899
The thesis of this chapter.

50
00:04:19.179 --> 00:04:20.501
Failures are normal.

51
00:04:20.781 --> 00:04:30.915
Outages are what happens when the system's reaction to a failure makes it worse: retries, queues and waiting amplify a small problem into a self-sustaining one.

52
00:04:31.195 --> 00:04:33.776
Resilience patterns cap that amplification.

53
00:04:34.056 --> 00:04:42.397
Bound every wait, every queue, every retry and every caller's share, and decide in advance what the user sees when a dependency is gone.

54
00:04:43.747 --> 00:04:46.051
