134
00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.145
Part 2.

135
00:07:24.425 --> 00:07:26.302
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

136
00:07:27.552 --> 00:07:29.984
Intuition: a dark store on sale day.

137
00:07:30.264 --> 00:07:36.259
Timeouts: a picker who can't find an item in 2 minutes moves on instead of blocking the order forever.

138
00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:43.024
Retries with jitter: when the billing system reboots, 20 riders shouldn't all tap retry at the same second.

139
00:07:43.304 --> 00:07:46.632
Each waits a random few seconds, so the counter isn't mobbed.

140
00:07:46.912 --> 00:07:54.592
Circuit breaker: after the third time the freezer door jams, the supervisor puts a sign on it, freezer out, use the backup.

141
00:07:54.872 --> 00:07:57.432
Nobody walks over to try again every minute.

142
00:07:57.712 --> 00:07:59.696
After 10 minutes someone checks it once.

143
00:08:01.046 --> 00:08:01.899
Bulkhead:

144
00:08:02.179 --> 00:08:08.430
2 of the 8 pickers handle only frozen goods, so a freezer problem can't pull all 8 pickers into it.

145
00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:16.177
Rate limiting: each B2B customer can place at most 50 orders a minute, so one bulk buyer can't starve everyone else.

146
00:08:16.457 --> 00:08:29.235
Load shedding: when the queue at the packing station is longer than packers can clear before the 10-minute delivery promise expires, the store stops accepting new slots rather than accepting orders it will deliver late.

147
00:08:29.515 --> 00:08:36.790
Graceful degradation: if the scale for weighing produce breaks, sell produce by the piece rather than stopping produce sales.

148
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.740
The amplification problem.

149
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:44.948
A dependency fails or slows, and then the system's reactions multiply the load:

150
00:08:45.228 --> 00:08:48.407
Retries: every failed call becomes 2 to 3 calls.

151
00:08:48.687 --> 00:08:52.847
At every layer, that's 27x (Ch 17 L2 C).

152
00:08:53.127 --> 00:08:59.847
Queues: requests pile up and wait longer than callers do, so the server works on requests nobody wants (L4).

153
00:09:00.127 --> 00:09:07.081
Waiting callers: threads and connections are held (Little's law, Ch 17 L1), and callers fail too.

154
00:09:07.361 --> 00:09:12.396
Rejection isn't free: accepting, parsing and rejecting still costs capacity.

155
00:09:12.676 --> 00:09:17.028
An overloaded server can end up serving fewer requests than its capacity.

156
00:09:18.378 --> 00:09:24.906
When those loops keep the system overloaded after the trigger has gone, it's a metastable failure (Bronson et al.

157
00:09:25.186 --> 00:09:26.637
2021).

158
00:09:26.917 --> 00:09:38.159
L1 models it: once the server spends capacity rejecting retries, synchronized retry schedules never get all 1,000 clients served in 100 simulated seconds.

159
00:09:39.509 --> 00:09:40.704
Timeouts (recap).

160
00:09:40.984 --> 00:09:46.317
Ch 17 covers timeouts in depth: from budgets, with deadlines propagated.

161
00:09:46.597 --> 00:09:55.024
In resilience terms, a timeout is what turns a slow failure into a fast failure that the other patterns (fallback, breaker) can act on.

162
00:09:55.304 --> 00:09:57.523
Without timeouts, none of the rest works.

163
00:09:58.873 --> 00:10:01.411
Retries with exponential backoff and jitter.

164
00:10:01.691 --> 00:10:15.601
When to retry: transient failures (connection reset, a 503 from one instance, a lock timeout), idempotent operations (or with idempotency keys, Ch 20), and while the deadline still allows it.

165
00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:16.542
How:

166
00:10:16.822 --> 00:10:20.363
Exponential backoff: wait base times 2^attempt, capped.

167
00:10:20.643 --> 00:10:25.721
Jitter spreads retries out in time, so clients that failed together don't retry together:

168
00:10:26.001 --> 00:10:28.603
Full jitter: wait random(0, backoff).

169
00:10:29.953 --> 00:10:34.540
Equal jitter: wait backoff or 2 + random(0, backoff or 2).

170
00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:41.732
Bounded attempts (2 to 3) and a retry budget (for example, retries at most 10 % of requests per client).

171
00:10:42.012 --> 00:10:43.228
Beyond that, fail.

172
00:10:43.508 --> 00:10:46.239
Honour Retry-After / 429.

173
00:10:46.519 --> 00:10:52.620
Don't retry non-retryable errors (400, 401, 404, validation).

174
00:10:52.900 --> 00:11:01.135
L1 simulated 1,000 clients failing together against a server that spends 0.25 of a slot to reject a call:

