202
00:11:48.064 --> 00:11:56.768
Plain exponential backoff still fails here: the clients back off together and arrive together again, every time above the server's capacity.

203
00:11:57.048 --> 00:11:59.245
Jitter is what breaks the synchronization.

204
00:11:59.525 --> 00:12:05.669
And retries at one layer only (Ch 17): the gateway or the first caller, not every hop.

205
00:12:07.019 --> 00:12:08.129
Circuit breaker.

206
00:12:08.409 --> 00:12:12.419
A circuit breaker wraps calls to a dependency and tracks their outcomes:

207
00:12:12.699 --> 00:12:14.043
CLOSED: calls flow.

208
00:12:14.323 --> 00:12:21.363
Outcomes are recorded in a sliding window (count-based: the last N calls; time-based: the last N seconds).

209
00:12:21.643 --> 00:12:31.947
OPEN: when the failure rate (or slow-call rate) exceeds a threshold, after a minimum number of calls, calls fail fast without touching the dependency, and the fallback runs.

210
00:12:32.227 --> 00:12:35.299
HALFOPEN: after a wait, a few probe calls are allowed.

211
00:12:35.579 --> 00:12:38.822
If they succeed the breaker closes, and if not it reopens.

212
00:12:40.172 --> 00:12:52.055
L2 ran against a real HTTP pricing service that failed slowly (800 ms, then 500) from 2 s to 8 s, with callers at 50 req per second:

