229
00:13:39.573 --> 00:13:40.619
What this shows:

230
00:13:40.899 --> 00:13:50.136
The breaker protects the dependency (5x fewer calls while it's down, which helps it recover) and the caller (no threads waiting 800 ms).

231
00:13:50.416 --> 00:13:59.419
The cost is recovery latency: the probe at 7.8 s hit the last moments of the outage and reopened the breaker for another 2 s.

232
00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:08.000
Tune waitDurationInOpenState against how fast the dependency typically recovers, and consider exponential open durations with a cap.

233
00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:13.547
Record slow calls as failures too (slowCallDurationThreshold).

234
00:14:13.827 --> 00:14:18.520
A dependency that answers in 5 s is failing from the caller's point of view.

235
00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:25.033
Scope breakers correctly: per dependency and, where it matters, per endpoint or instance.

236
00:14:25.313 --> 00:14:29.921
One breaker for Razorpay would open for UPI when only cards are failing.

237
00:14:30.201 --> 00:14:37.497
A bug worth knowing: the first version of L2's breaker recorded the results of calls that were still in flight when it opened.

238
00:14:37.777 --> 00:14:46.353
Each late failure reopened the breaker and reset its timer, so it logged 40 OPEN transitions and stayed open far longer than configured.

239
00:14:46.633 --> 00:14:51.774
Late results from calls admitted before a state change must not drive the state machine.

240
00:14:52.054 --> 00:14:56.321
Resilience4j handles this, but hand-rolled breakers often don't.

241
00:14:57.671 --> 00:14:58.567
Bulkheads.

242
00:14:58.847 --> 00:15:05.396
A bulkhead limits the resources one dependency or workload can use, so failures stay in their compartment:

243
00:15:05.676 --> 00:15:09.665
Semaphore bulkhead: at most N concurrent calls to a dependency.

244
00:15:09.945 --> 00:15:12.911
Callers beyond N fail fast (or wait briefly).

245
00:15:13.191 --> 00:15:17.564
With virtual threads this is the natural form (Ch 17 L1).

246
00:15:17.844 --> 00:15:25.076
Thread-pool bulkhead: a dedicated pool per dependency, which isolates blocking calls in platform-thread stacks.

247
00:15:25.356 --> 00:15:27.148
Connection-pool per dependency:

248
00:15:27.428 --> 00:15:35.983
Ch 17's or cart failure (20 or 500) came from shared threads, and a per-dependency pool plus limit isolates it.

249
00:15:37.333 --> 00:15:46.613
At the infrastructure level: separate deployments or node pools for critical versus batch workloads, and separate database pools for OLTP versus reports.

250
00:15:46.893 --> 00:15:50.775
Cells (Ch 34) are bulkheads at the architecture level.

251
00:15:51.055 --> 00:15:57.775
Size bulkheads with Little's law (normal rate times p99 latency times 1.5 to 2).

252
00:15:58.055 --> 00:16:02.386
Too small rejects normal traffic, and too large doesn't protect anything.

253
00:16:03.736 --> 00:16:04.739
Rate limiting.

254
00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:12.336
Rate limiting caps how much a client or caller may send, protecting shared capacity and enforcing fairness or quotas.

255
