Per-pod limiters let 240 req per second through.

309
00:18:31.867 --> 00:18:34.832
Each pod is under its own 100 req per second.

310
00:18:35.112 --> 00:18:49.555
A shared token bucket in Redis (one atomic Lua script) held the total to 133 req per second over 3 s (the initial 100-token burst + 100 per second), split evenly across the pods.

311
00:18:50.905 --> 00:19:02.595
Options: a central store (Redis + Lua, Ch 09 or 39), approximate local limits = global or N with periodic rebalancing, or the gateway doing it.

312
00:19:02.875 --> 00:19:09.766
Decide what happens when the limiter store is down: fail open for protection limits, fail closed for paid quotas.

313
00:19:10.046 --> 00:19:17.022
Respond with 429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After, and include the limit headers (RateLimit-).

314
00:19:17.302 --> 00:19:20.011
Clients must back off (§2.4).

315
00:19:21.361 --> 00:19:23.367
Load shedding and admission control.

316
00:19:23.647 --> 00:19:27.444
When offered load exceeds capacity, something must be rejected.

317
00:19:27.724 --> 00:19:32.631
The question is whether it's rejected early and cheaply, or served late and uselessly.

318
00:19:32.911 --> 00:19:44.857
