351
00:20:55.224 --> 00:20:56.440
What the numbers mean:

352
00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:58.448
The unbounded queue collapses.

353
00:20:58.728 --> 00:21:08.840
Every request waits longer than the client, so the server spends all its capacity on responses nobody reads, and goodput drops to ~1 % of capacity.

354
00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.808
That's §1.1's inventory service.

355
00:21:12.088 --> 00:21:17.485
Bounding the queue, or dropping requests that already waited too long, keeps goodput at capacity.

356
00:21:17.765 --> 00:21:25.104
The rejected clients get a fast, honest 503 they can retry later with backoff, instead of a slow timeout.

357
00:21:25.384 --> 00:21:26.237
Techniques:

358
00:21:26.517 --> 00:21:29.931
Bounded queues and concurrency limits: reject when full.

359
00:21:31.281 --> 00:21:38.385
Deadline-aware dropping: skip requests whose caller's deadline has passed or is about to (Ch 17 deadlines).

360
00:21:38.665 --> 00:21:42.270
CoDel-style: drop when queueing delay stays above a target.

361
00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:47.222
LIFO under overload: serve the newest requests (their clients are still waiting).

362
00:21:47.502 --> 00:21:49.337
Used by some RPC stacks.

363
00:21:49.617 --> 00:21:59.601
Adaptive concurrency limits (TCP-like AIMD, Netflix concurrency-limits, Envoy adaptive concurrency): lower the limit when latency rises.

364
00:21:59.881 --> 00:22:03.465
Priority shedding: shed analytics and prefetch before checkout.

365
00:22:03.745 --> 00:22:07.393
Tag requests with a criticality class (Google's criticality).

366
00:22:08.743 --> 00:22:14.737
Shed at the edge, where it's cheapest: the gateway rate limits and rejects before work enters the system.

367
00:22:16.087 --> 00:22:18.199
Fallbacks and graceful degradation.

368
00:22:18.479 --> 00:22:20.399
A fallback is what you do instead.

369
00:22:20.679 --> 00:22:22.685
Its quality ranges from good to bad:

370
00:22:24.035 --> 00:22:25.379
Here is a quick table:

371
00:22:25.659 --> 00:22:27.579
Fallback, and Example, and Risk.

372
00:22:27.859 --> 00:22:29.288
Cached or stale data.

373
00:22:29.568 --> 00:22:30.443
Example:

374
00:22:30.723 --> 00:22:33.816
Last known stock badge, price cache with TTL.

375
00:22:34.096 --> 00:22:34.800
Risk:

376
00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.213
Staleness: show as of where it matters.

377
00:22:37.493 --> 00:22:39.520
Default or simplified behaviour.

378
00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:40.675
Example:

379
00:22:40.955 --> 00:22:43.856
Price without coupon; generic recommendations.

380
00:22:44.136 --> 00:22:44.840
Risk:

381
00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:47.061
Business impact must be accepted.

382
00:22:47.341 --> 00:22:48.813
Alternative provider.

383
00:22:49.093 --> 00:22:49.968
Example:

384
00:22:50.248 --> 00:22:53.149
Second SMS vendor; another payment method.

385
00:22:53.429 --> 00:22:54.133
Risk:

386
00:22:54.413 --> 00:22:55.757
Cost, consistency.

387
00:22:56.037 --> 00:22:57.040
Queue for later.

388
00:22:57.320 --> 00:22:58.195
Example:

389
00:22:58.475 --> 00:23:01.973
Accept the order, confirm by SMS when payment settles.

390
00:23:02.253 --> 00:23:02.957
Risk:

391
00:23:03.237 --> 00:23:04.965
UX and state complexity.

392
00:23:05.245 --> 00:23:07.101
Fail fast with a clear message.

393
00:23:07.381 --> 00:23:08.256
Example:

394
00:23:08.536 --> 00:23:10.712
Payments are slow right now, try UPI.

395
00:23:10.992 --> 00:23:11.696
Risk:

396
00:23:11.976 --> 00:23:13.960
Honest, often the best for money.

397
00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:16.718
Silently wrong data.

398
00:23:16.998 --> 00:23:17.873
Example:

399
00:23:18.153 --> 00:23:20.926
Default price 0, or in stock for checkout.

400
00:23:21.206 --> 00:23:21.910
Risk:

401
00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:24.195
Never for correctness-critical paths.

402
00:23:25.545 --> 00:23:27.273
Graceful degradation plan:

403
00:23:27.553 --> 00:23:36.748
Each feature is classified as critical (checkout, payment, order status) or degradable (recommendations, reviews, ETA precision, coupons).

404
00:23:37.028 --> 00:23:43.620
Kill switches or feature flags turn degradable features off under load (brownout), and it's tested regularly.

405
00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.887
Rule: fallbacks must never be riskier than failures.

406
00:23:47.167 --> 00:23:52.671
A fallback path used once a year is untested code, so exercise fallbacks in game days.

407
00:23:54.021 --> 00:23:56.495
Putting the patterns together (and in what order).

408
00:23:56.775 --> 00:23:58.375
The order of wrapping matters.

409
00:23:58.655 --> 00:24:01.514
Resilience4j's default aspect order is:

410
00:24:01.794 --> 00:24:06.018
Retry ( CircuitBreaker ( RateLimiter ( TimeLimiter ( Bulkhead ( call ) ) ) ) ).

411
00:24:06.298 --> 00:24:10.458
The bulkhead is innermost: it limits the concurrency of actual calls.

412
00:24:10.738 --> 00:24:12.701
The time limiter bounds each attempt.

413
00:24:12.981 --> 00:24:18.634
The circuit breaker sees each attempt's outcome (including timeouts) and fails fast when open.

414
00:24:19.984 --> 00:24:27.963
Retry is outermost: it retries attempts, and when the breaker is open, the retry gets CallNotPermittedException immediately.

415
00:24:28.243 --> 00:24:33.427
Configure it not to retry that exception, or you're retrying against an open breaker.

416
00:24:33.707 --> 00:24:37.952
The fallback is applied at the outermost level, after retries are exhausted.

417
00:24:39.302 --> 00:24:42.054
Testing resilience: chaos and game days.

418
00:24:42.334 --> 00:24:53.683
Fault injection: latency, errors, dropped connections (mesh fault injection, Toxiproxy, Chaos Mesh, AWS FIS), process kills, and DB failovers (Ch 10 lab).

419
00:24:53.963 --> 00:25:05.334
Hypothesis-driven experiments: if inventory adds 2 s latency, checkout success stays ≥ 99 % and catalog pages show cached badges.

420
00:25:05.614 --> 00:25:10.115
Measure against SLOs, and keep the blast radius small with a quick abort.

421
00:25:10.395 --> 00:25:20.209
Game days with the on-call team, including the runbooks (§1.1's scale checkout to zero should be a documented, tested lever, not a lucky guess).

422
00:25:21.559 --> 00:25:24.311
Load tests beyond capacity verify shedding.

423
00:25:24.591 --> 00:25:28.559
Many systems are only tested up to their capacity, never past it.

