00:27:54.145 --> 00:27:54.785
1.

470
00:27:55.065 --> 00:27:56.473
Context and Problem.

471
00:27:56.753 --> 00:28:07.697
A 25-second database failover became a 41-minute outage through retry storms, unbounded queues, missing breakers and no fallbacks (§1.1).

472
00:28:07.977 --> 00:28:08.681
2.

473
00:28:08.961 --> 00:28:10.305
Goals or Non-Goals.

474
00:28:10.585 --> 00:28:27.908
Goals: recovery within 60 s of a dependency recovering; goodput ≥ 90 % of capacity under 2x overload; degradable features degrade instead of failing critical flows; no single client can exceed its share of a shared dependency.

475
00:28:28.188 --> 00:28:36.871
Non-goals: eliminating dependency failures (Ch 27 covers redundancy); exactly-once semantics (Ch 13).

476
00:28:38.221 --> 00:28:38.967
3.

477
00:28:39.247 --> 00:28:40.207
Requirements.

478
00:28:41.557 --> 00:28:42.901
Here is a quick table:

479
00:28:43.181 --> 00:28:44.653
ID, and Requirement.

480
00:28:44.933 --> 00:28:45.979
NFR-1:

481
00:28:46.259 --> 00:28:54.728
Retries: max 1 at the gateway (idempotent), clients use exponential backoff with full jitter, retry budget at most 10 %.

482
00:28:55.008 --> 00:28:56.053
NFR-2:

483
00:28:56.333 --> 00:29:02.605
Every outbound dependency has a timeout, a breaker (failure + slow-call thresholds) and a bulkhead.

484
00:29:02.885 --> 00:29:03.952
NFR-3:

485
00:29:04.232 --> 00:29:10.227
Every service ingress has bounded concurrency or queues and drops requests past their deadline.

486
00:29:10.507 --> 00:29:11.595
NFR-4:

487
00:29:11.875 --> 00:29:21.709
Under 2x offered load in a load test, goodput ≥ 90 % of capacity and p99 of successes at most 2x normal.

488
00:29:21.989 --> 00:29:23.035
NFR-5:

489
00:29:23.315 --> 00:29:30.589
Each feature is classified critical or degradable, and degradable features have a tested fallback and a brownout flag.

490
00:29:31.939 --> 00:29:33.027
NFR-6:

491
00:29:33.307 --> 00:29:40.134
Shared dependencies with limits (vendor APIs, legacy ERP) are protected by a global limiter.

492
