00:47:14.051 --> 00:47:14.926
L4:

730
00:47:15.206 --> 00:47:17.403
Fast 503s for the excess.

731
00:47:18.753 --> 00:47:19.692
Part 6.

732
00:47:19.972 --> 00:47:21.252
Production Concerns.

733
00:47:22.502 --> 00:47:23.654
Failure modes.

734
00:47:23.934 --> 00:47:25.278
Here is a quick table:

735
00:47:25.558 --> 00:47:29.441
Failure, and Detection signal, and Blast radius, and Mitigation.

736
00:47:29.721 --> 00:47:31.406
Retry storm after a blip.

737
00:47:31.686 --> 00:47:32.881
Detection signal:

738
00:47:33.161 --> 00:47:36.339
Request rate ≫ user traffic; retries per request up.

739
00:47:36.619 --> 00:47:37.835
Blast radius:

740
00:47:38.115 --> 00:47:40.910
Dependency can't recover (L1: never).

741
00:47:41.190 --> 00:47:42.193
Mitigation:

742
00:47:42.473 --> 00:47:45.801
One retry layer, jitter, retry budgets, breakers.

743
00:47:46.081 --> 00:47:47.531
Metastable overload.

744
00:47:47.811 --> 00:47:49.006
Detection signal:

745
00:47:49.286 --> 00:47:51.761
Overload persists after the trigger is gone.

746
00:47:52.041 --> 00:47:53.257
Blast radius:

747
00:47:53.537 --> 00:47:54.475
Whole flow.

748
00:47:54.755 --> 00:47:55.758
Mitigation:

749
00:47:56.038 --> 00:47:59.451
Shedding, retry budgets, a documented load-drop lever.

750
00:47:59.731 --> 00:48:00.905
Unbounded queues.

751
00:48:01.185 --> 00:48:02.379
Detection signal:

752
00:48:02.659 --> 00:48:06.627
Queue depth and wait time growing; work done for timed-out callers.

753
00:48:06.907 --> 00:48:08.123
Blast radius:

754
00:48:08.403 --> 00:48:10.003
Goodput collapse (L4:

755
00:48:10.283 --> 00:48:11.649
4 req per second).

756
00:48:11.929 --> 00:48:12.931
Mitigation:

757
00:48:13.211 --> 00:48:15.643
Bounded queues, deadline-aware dropping.

758
00:48:15.923 --> 00:48:17.395
Breaker thresholds wrong.

759
00:48:17.675 --> 00:48:18.870
Detection signal:

760
00:48:19.150 --> 00:48:21.305
Breaker flapping, or never opening.

761
00:48:21.585 --> 00:48:22.801
Blast radius:

762
00:48:23.081 --> 00:48:25.129
False outages, or no protection.

763
00:48:25.409 --> 00:48:26.411
Mitigation:

764
00:48:26.691 --> 00:48:30.147
Tune from data; slow-call rules; alert on transitions.

765
00:48:31.497 --> 00:48:32.927
Breaker opens too long.

766
00:48:33.207 --> 00:48:34.401
Detection signal:

767
00:48:34.681 --> 00:48:39.289
Recovery lag after the dependency heals (L2: +2.7 s).

768
00:48:39.569 --> 00:48:40.785
Blast radius:

769
00:48:41.065 --> 00:48:42.495
Longer degraded mode.

770
00:48:42.775 --> 00:48:43.777
Mitigation:

771
00:48:44.057 --> 00:48:46.809
Shorter wait with backoff, more half-open probes.

772
00:48:47.089 --> 00:48:48.220
Fallback bugs.

773
00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:49.695
Detection signal:

774
00:48:49.975 --> 00:48:52.513
Errors in rarely used paths during incidents.

775
00:48:52.793 --> 00:48:54.009
Blast radius:

776
00:48:54.289 --> 00:48:56.060
Worse than the original failure.

777
00:48:56.340 --> 00:48:57.343
Mitigation:

778
00:48:57.623 --> 00:48:59.607
Exercise fallbacks in game days.

779
00:48:59.887 --> 00:49:01.487
Wrong default in a fallback.

780
00:49:01.767 --> 00:49:02.961
Detection signal:

781
00:49:03.241 --> 00:49:05.332
In stock when it isn't, price 0.

782
00:49:05.612 --> 00:49:06.828
Blast radius:

783
00:49:07.108 --> 00:49:08.431
Correctness or money.

784
00:49:08.711 --> 00:49:09.713
Mitigation:

785
00:49:09.993 --> 00:49:14.132
Critical paths fail clearly; degradable ones use stale-but-true data.

786
00:49:14.412 --> 00:49:16.801
Local limiters on a shared dependency.

787
00:49:17.081 --> 00:49:18.276
Detection signal:

788
00:49:18.556 --> 00:49:21.308
Dependency exceeds its limit as pods scale.

789
00:49:21.588 --> 00:49:22.804
Blast radius:

790
00:49:23.084 --> 00:49:25.175
Vendor throttling or ban (L3:

791
00:49:25.455 --> 00:49:28.527
240 versus 100 req per second).

792
00:49:28.807 --> 00:49:29.809
Mitigation:

793
00:49:30.089 --> 00:49:32.244
Global limiter in Redis or gateway.

794
00:49:33.594 --> 00:49:35.087
Rate limiter store down.

795
00:49:35.367 --> 00:49:36.562
Detection signal:

796
00:49:36.842 --> 00:49:37.887
Limiter errors.

797
00:49:38.167 --> 00:49:39.383
Blast radius:

798
00:49:39.663 --> 00:49:42.927
Unprotected dependency, or all traffic rejected.

799
00:49:43.207 --> 00:49:44.210
Mitigation:

800
00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:47.455
Explicit fail-open or closed policy per limit.

801
00:49:48.805 --> 00:49:49.893
Observability.

802
00:49:50.173 --> 00:50:01.693
Per dependency: breaker state (a gauge), transitions (events), failure and slow-call rates, calls not permitted, bulkhead available permits and rejections, fallback count by reason.

803
00:50:01.973 --> 00:50:10.443
Per service: in-flight requests, queue wait time, requests shed by reason (overload, deadline, rate limit), and goodput versus throughput.

804
00:50:10.723 --> 00:50:13.752
A gap between them is the signature of wasted work.

805
00:50:14.032 --> 00:50:18.512
Retries: attempts per request and retry-budget usage, split by layer.

806
00:50:18.792 --> 00:50:19.795
Rate limits:

807
00:50:20.075 --> 00:50:25.195
429s per client and per limit, limiter latency, and the store's health.

808
00:50:26.545 --> 00:50:30.875
Dashboards show all of these per dependency on one screen during incidents.

809
00:50:31.155 --> 00:50:35.443
Resilience4j exports Micrometer metrics for its components.

810
00:50:36.793 --> 00:50:37.732
Security.

811
00:50:38.012 --> 00:50:43.751
Rate limits are also a security control (credential stuffing, OTP abuse, scraping).

812
00:50:44.031 --> 00:50:50.580
Apply them per identity, per IP and per device, at the edge (Ch 23, Ch 39).

813
00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:54.913
Load shedding and rate limiting help absorb layer-7 DDoS.

814
00:50:55.193 --> 00:50:58.287
Pair them with CDN or WAF protections.

815
00:50:58.567 --> 00:51:09.831
Error responses under shedding (503, 429) must not leak internals, and Retry-After should be jittered server-side to avoid synchronizing clients.

816
00:51:10.111 --> 00:51:15.209
Brownout flags and kill switches are powerful: protect them with RBAC and audit logs.

817
00:51:16.559 --> 00:51:18.223
Performance and tuning knobs.

818
00:51:18.503 --> 00:51:19.847
Here is a quick table:

819
00:51:20.127 --> 00:51:22.111
Knob, and Default here, and Effect.

820
00:51:22.391 --> 00:51:24.589
Retry attempts or backoff or jitter.

821
00:51:24.869 --> 00:51:25.786
Default here:

822
00:51:26.066 --> 00:51:30.226
1 to 2 or 50 ms times 2ⁿ or full or 50 %.

823
00:51:30.506 --> 00:51:31.295
Effect:

824
00:51:31.575 --> 00:51:33.495
Transient recovery versus load.

825
00:51:33.775 --> 00:51:34.949
Retry budget.

826
00:51:35.229 --> 00:51:37.895
Default here: at most 10 % of requests.

827
00:51:38.175 --> 00:51:38.965
Effect:

828
00:51:39.245 --> 00:51:40.525
Caps amplification.

829
00:51:40.805 --> 00:51:42.554
Breaker window or min calls.

830
00:51:42.834 --> 00:51:43.751
Default here:

831
00:51:44.031 --> 00:51:45.141
20 or 10.

832
00:51:45.421 --> 00:51:46.210
Effect:

833
00:51:46.490 --> 00:51:48.282
Reaction speed versus noise.

834
00:51:48.562 --> 00:51:50.503
Failure or slow-call thresholds.

835
00:51:50.783 --> 00:51:51.701
Default here:

836
00:51:51.981 --> 00:51:54.626
50 % / 1 s at 50 %.

837
00:51:54.906 --> 00:51:55.695
Effect:

838
00:51:55.975 --> 00:51:57.021
Sensitivity.

839
00:51:57.301 --> 00:51:58.666
Wait in open state.

840
00:51:58.946 --> 00:51:59.863
Default here:

841
00:52:00.143 --> 00:52:02.085
5 s (10 s for optional).

842
00:52:02.365 --> 00:52:03.154
Effect:

843
00:52:03.434 --> 00:52:06.037
Protection versus recovery lag (L2).

844
00:52:06.317 --> 00:52:07.447
Bulkhead size.

845
00:52:07.727 --> 00:52:08.645
Default here:

846
00:52:08.925 --> 00:52:11.207
Little's law times 1.5 to 2.

847
00:52:11.487 --> 00:52:12.277
Effect:

848
00:52:12.557 --> 00:52:14.882
Isolation versus false rejections.

849
00:52:15.162 --> 00:52:17.103
Ingress queue bound or max wait.

850
00:52:17.383 --> 00:52:22.503
Default here: ~100 ms of capacity or 250 ms.

851
00:52:22.783 --> 00:52:23.573
Effect:

852
00:52:23.853 --> 00:52:25.815
Goodput under overload (L4).

853
00:52:26.095 --> 00:52:27.802
Token bucket rate or burst.

854
00:52:28.082 --> 00:52:28.999
Default here:

855
00:52:29.279 --> 00:52:30.346
Per client tier.

856
00:52:30.626 --> 00:52:31.415
Effect:

857
00:52:31.695 --> 00:52:34.213
Fairness versus burst tolerance (L3).

858
00:52:35.563 --> 00:52:36.288
Cost.

859
00:52:36.568 --> 00:52:46.744
Headroom is the cheapest resilience: running at 50 to 60 % of capacity at peak absorbs retries and failovers without triggering the amplification loops.

860
00:52:47.024 --> 00:52:49.499
Balance it against cost (Ch 30).

861
00:52:49.779 --> 00:52:56.691
Shedding and breakers reduce the capacity needed to survive incidents, because the system doesn't have to serve waste.

862
00:52:56.971 --> 00:53:03.755
Fallback caches cost memory and consistency effort, so budget them per feature in the degradation catalogue.

863
00:53:04.035 --> 00:53:10.605
Game days cost engineering time, and they are much cheaper than the 41-minute outage in §1.1.

864
00:53:11.955 --> 00:53:12.894
Part 7.

865
00:53:13.174 --> 00:53:14.881
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

866
00:53:16.131 --> 00:53:16.771
1.

867
00:53:17.051 --> 00:53:18.992
Retries without backoff and jitter.

868
00:53:20.342 --> 00:53:21.857
Here is the Java code.

869
00:53:22.137 --> 00:53:39.417
It runs to 17 lines; the full listing is in the chapter. before: synchronized retries keep the dependency above capacity (L1: never recovers). after: bounded attempts, exponential backoff with full jitter, only within the deadline, only if idempotent.

870
00:53:42.267 --> 00:53:42.971
2.

871
00:53:43.251 --> 00:53:44.872
Retrying at every layer.

872
00:53:45.152 --> 00:53:45.963
Symptom:

873
00:53:46.243 --> 00:53:50.637
27x load on the failing leaf (Ch 17 L2 C).

874
00:53:50.917 --> 00:53:51.643
Fix:

875
00:53:51.923 --> 00:53:53.800
One retry layer with a budget.

876
00:53:54.080 --> 00:53:54.827
3.

877
00:53:55.107 --> 00:53:57.816
Unbounded queues (we never drop requests).

878
00:53:58.096 --> 00:53:58.907
Symptom:

879
00:53:59.187 --> 00:54:01.747
Goodput collapses under overload (L4:

880
00:54:02.027 --> 00:54:05.632
4 req per second), and the server works for clients that left.

881
00:54:05.912 --> 00:54:06.637
Fix:

882
00:54:06.917 --> 00:54:10.864
Bounded queues, deadline-aware dropping, fast 503s.

883
00:54:11.144 --> 00:54:11.848
4.

884
00:54:12.128 --> 00:54:14.304
No circuit breaker on slow failures.

885
00:54:14.584 --> 00:54:15.395
Symptom:

886
00:54:15.675 --> 00:54:21.883
Every caller waits for the full timeout, and threads pile up (L2: p50 802 ms).

887
00:54:22.163 --> 00:54:22.888
Fix:

888
00:54:23.168 --> 00:54:26.005
A breaker with slow-call thresholds, and a fallback.

889
00:54:27.355 --> 00:54:28.123
5.

890
00:54:28.403 --> 00:54:30.302
Breakers that count late results.

891
00:54:30.582 --> 00:54:31.393
Symptom:

892
00:54:31.673 --> 00:54:35.918
The breaker keeps reopening and resetting its timer (L2's first version:

893
00:54:36.198 --> 00:54:38.651
40 OPEN transitions in under a second).

894
00:54:38.931 --> 00:54:39.657
Fix:

895
00:54:39.937 --> 00:54:45.121
Ignore outcomes of calls admitted before the current state, or use a library that does.

896
00:54:45.401 --> 00:54:46.147
6.

897
00:54:46.427 --> 00:54:47.643
Fallbacks that lie.

898
00:54:47.923 --> 00:54:48.734
Symptom:

899
00:54:49.014 --> 00:54:52.897
The checkout fallback returns in stock, and the order can't be fulfilled.

900
00:54:53.177 --> 00:54:53.902
Fix:

901
00:54:54.182 --> 00:54:58.577
Stale-but-true data for degradable features, clear errors for critical ones.

902
00:54:58.857 --> 00:54:59.603
7.

903
00:54:59.883 --> 00:55:02.102
Fixed-window rate limits for protection.

904
00:55:02.382 --> 00:55:06.179
Symptom: ~2x the limit at window boundaries (L3:

905
00:55:06.459 --> 00:55:08.955
175 versus 100).

906
00:55:09.235 --> 00:55:09.961
Fix:

907
00:55:10.241 --> 00:55:12.203
Sliding window or token bucket.

908
00:55:13.553 --> 00:55:14.236
8.

909
00:55:14.516 --> 00:55:16.799
Per-instance limits for a shared dependency.

910
00:55:17.079 --> 00:55:17.889
Symptom:

911
00:55:18.169 --> 00:55:22.457
Autoscaling multiplies traffic to a vendor with a hard limit (L3:

912
00:55:22.737 --> 00:55:25.788
240 req per second versus 100).

913
00:55:26.068 --> 00:55:26.793
Fix:

914
00:55:27.073 --> 00:55:29.569
A global limiter (Redis Lua, gateway).

915
00:55:29.849 --> 00:55:30.596
9.

916
00:55:30.876 --> 00:55:34.012
Shared thread or connection pools across dependencies.

917
00:55:34.292 --> 00:55:35.103
Symptom:

918
00:55:35.383 --> 00:55:40.972
One slow dependency takes down unrelated features (Ch 17 L2 A).

919
00:55:41.252 --> 00:55:41.977
Fix:

920
00:55:42.257 --> 00:55:43.751
Bulkheads per dependency.

921
00:55:44.031 --> 00:55:44.735
10.

922
00:55:45.015 --> 00:55:46.807
Resilience that's never tested.

923
00:55:47.087 --> 00:55:47.897
Symptom:

924
00:55:48.177 --> 00:55:51.612
Fallbacks and breakers fail during the first real incident.

925
00:55:51.892 --> 00:55:52.617
Fix:

926
00:55:52.897 --> 00:55:58.359
Chaos experiments and game days with explicit hypotheses and abort criteria.

927
00:55:59.709 --> 00:56:00.583
Part 8.

928
00:56:00.863 --> 00:56:02.207
Interview Q and A.

929
00:56:03.457 --> 00:56:04.289
Beginner.

930
00:56:04.569 --> 00:56:05.593
Question 1.

931
00:56:05.873 --> 00:56:07.260
What is a circuit breaker?

932
00:56:07.540 --> 00:56:08.905
Thirty-second answer:

933
00:56:09.185 --> 00:56:12.620
A wrapper around calls to a dependency that tracks failures.

934
00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:22.863
When the failure (or slow-call) rate crosses a threshold, it opens and immediately fails further calls, usually returning a fallback, without calling the dependency.

935
00:56:23.143 --> 00:56:28.391
After a wait it lets a few test calls through (half-open) and closes again if they succeed.

936
00:56:29.741 --> 00:56:30.743
Question 2.

937
00:56:31.023 --> 00:56:32.623
Why add jitter to retries?

938
00:56:32.903 --> 00:56:34.269
Thirty-second answer:

939
00:56:34.549 --> 00:56:41.503
Clients that fail together will otherwise retry together, creating waves of load that keep the dependency overloaded.

940
00:56:41.783 --> 00:56:46.861
Jitter randomizes the wait, spreading retries out so the dependency can recover.

941
00:56:48.211 --> 00:56:49.256
Question 3.

942
00:56:49.536 --> 00:56:50.667
What is a bulkhead?

943
00:56:50.947 --> 00:56:52.312
Thirty-second answer:

944
00:56:52.592 --> 00:57:05.541
A limit on the resources (threads, connections, concurrent calls) one dependency or workload can use, so a failure in one part can't exhaust resources for everything else, like compartments in a ship.

945
00:57:06.891 --> 00:57:07.958
Question 4.

946
00:57:08.238 --> 00:57:10.606
What's rate limiting, and where do you apply it?

947
00:57:10.886 --> 00:57:12.251
Thirty-second answer:

948
00:57:12.531 --> 00:57:16.734
Capping how many requests a client, key or caller can make per time unit.

949
00:57:17.014 --> 00:57:21.217
It protects capacity, enforces fairness and quotas, and blunts abuse.

950
00:57:21.497 --> 00:57:30.329
Apply it at the edge (gateway, per API key per user or IP) and in front of shared dependencies with hard limits (vendor APIs).

951
00:57:31.679 --> 00:57:32.681
Intermediate.

952
00:57:32.961 --> 00:57:34.071
Question 5.

953
00:57:34.351 --> 00:57:37.444
Compare token bucket, leaky bucket and sliding window.

954
00:57:37.724 --> 00:57:39.089
Thirty-second answer:

955
00:57:39.369 --> 00:57:48.287
Token bucket: tokens refill at rate r up to capacity b, and each request spends one, so it allows bursts up to b and averages r.

956
00:57:48.567 --> 00:57:56.652
Leaky bucket (as a queue): requests leave at a constant rate, which shapes traffic by delaying it and rejects only when the queue is full.

957
00:57:56.932 --> 00:58:04.036
Sliding window: counts requests in the last window (exact log or weighted counter) for strict N per window.

958
00:58:04.316 --> 00:58:07.089
Fixed windows allow ~2x at boundaries.

959
00:58:08.439 --> 00:58:09.549
Question 6.

960
00:58:09.829 --> 00:58:11.535
How do you tune a circuit breaker?

961
00:58:11.815 --> 00:58:13.181
Thirty-second answer:

962
00:58:13.461 --> 00:58:33.301
From traffic and failure data: a window big enough to avoid noise but small enough to react within a second or two, a minimum number of calls, a failure-rate threshold ( about 50 %) plus slow-call thresholds, an open duration tied to how fast the dependency typically recovers, and a few half-open probes.

963
00:58:33.581 --> 00:58:35.607
Exclude business errors from failures.

964
00:58:36.957 --> 00:58:38.045
Question 7.

965
00:58:38.325 --> 00:58:39.691
What makes a good fallback?

966
00:58:39.971 --> 00:58:41.336
Thirty-second answer:

967
00:58:41.616 --> 00:58:51.259
One that's safe and honest: stale-but-true cached data, simplified behaviour the business has accepted, an alternative provider, or a queue-for-later path.

968
00:58:51.539 --> 00:58:56.808
For critical correctness (money, stock at checkout), a clear failure is the right fallback.

969
00:58:57.088 --> 00:58:58.368
Never invent data.

970
00:58:59.718 --> 00:59:00.742
Question 8.

971
00:59:01.022 --> 00:59:05.161
In what order do you apply retry, circuit breaker, bulkhead and timeout?

972
00:59:05.441 --> 00:59:06.806
Thirty-second answer:

973
00:59:07.086 --> 00:59:08.835
Resilience4j's order:

974
00:59:09.115 --> 00:59:14.641
Retry wraps CircuitBreaker, which wraps RateLimiter, TimeLimiter and Bulkhead around the call.

975
00:59:14.921 --> 00:59:24.585
The bulkhead limits real concurrency, the time limiter bounds each attempt, the breaker sees each attempt's result, and retry decides whether to try again.

976
00:59:24.865 --> 00:59:28.513
Don't retry CallNotPermittedException from an open breaker.

977
00:59:28.793 --> 00:59:30.542
The fallback applies outermost.

978
00:59:31.892 --> 00:59:32.959
Question 9.

979
00:59:33.239 --> 00:59:35.073
Why do retries need a budget?

980
00:59:35.353 --> 00:59:36.719
Thirty-second answer:

981
00:59:36.999 --> 00:59:46.705
Because under a real outage every request fails, so unbounded retry 3 times means 3 to 4x the load exactly when the dependency is weakest.

982
00:59:46.985 --> 00:59:57.737
A budget (for example, retries at most 10 % of requests over a window) allows retries for sporadic failures and stops them automatically during widespread failure.

983
00:59:59.087 --> 00:59:59.855
Senior.

984
01:00:00.135 --> 01:00:01.181
Question 10.

985
01:00:01.461 --> 01:00:05.087
What is load shedding, and why is an unbounded queue dangerous?

986
01:00:05.367 --> 01:00:06.733
Thirty-second answer:

987
01:00:07.013 --> 01:00:15.823
Load shedding rejects excess work early and cheaply when demand exceeds capacity, so the capacity serves requests that can still succeed.

988
01:00:16.103 --> 01:00:24.786
An unbounded queue accepts everything, requests wait longer than their callers, and the server spends its capacity on responses nobody reads.

989
01:00:25.066 --> 01:00:28.543
Goodput collapses while CPU stays at 100 %.

990
01:00:29.893 --> 01:00:31.024
Question 11.

991
01:00:31.304 --> 01:00:33.117
Explain metastable failures.

992
01:00:33.397 --> 01:00:34.763
Thirty-second answer:

993
01:00:35.043 --> 01:00:46.157
A state where a system stays overloaded after the trigger (a failover, a spike) has gone, because a feedback loop (retries, queue buildup, cache misses) keeps load above capacity.

994
01:00:46.437 --> 01:00:50.171
Recovery needs removing load, not fixing the original fault.

995
01:00:51.521 --> 01:00:52.609
Question 12.

996
01:00:52.889 --> 01:00:56.281
How do you implement a global rate limit across many pods?

997
01:00:56.561 --> 01:00:57.926
Thirty-second answer:

998
01:00:58.206 --> 01:01:07.550
Keep the limiter state in a shared store and update it atomically, typically a token bucket or sliding window in a Redis Lua script keyed by the limit's scope.

999
01:01:07.830 --> 01:01:10.177
Or enforce it at a single gateway tier.

1000
01:01:10.457 --> 01:01:16.259
Decide fail-open versus fail-closed when the store is unavailable, and keep the store call cheap.

1001
01:01:17.609 --> 01:01:18.889
Question 13.

1002
01:01:19.169 --> 01:01:22.881
How do you make the catalog page survive an inventory outage?

1003
01:01:23.161 --> 01:01:24.527
Thirty-second answer:

1004
01:01:24.807 --> 01:01:27.388
Classify the stock badge as degradable.

1005
01:01:27.668 --> 01:01:36.543
Call inventory with a short timeout behind a breaker and bulkhead, and fall back to a cached badge (stale-but-true with an age limit) or hide it.

1006
01:01:36.823 --> 01:01:40.535
Separately, keep checkout's reserve call strict: it fails clearly.

1007
01:01:40.815 --> 01:01:49.220
Better still, feed badges by events into a local cache (Ch 15), so the page doesn't call inventory synchronously at all.

1008
01:01:50.570 --> 01:01:51.765
Question 14.

1009
01:01:52.045 --> 01:01:54.114
How would you test your resilience patterns?

1010
01:01:54.394 --> 01:01:55.759
Thirty-second answer:

1011
01:01:56.039 --> 01:02:00.178
Unit tests for the state machines (breaker transitions, limiter math).

1012
01:02:00.458 --> 01:02:07.285
Integration tests with fault injection (latency, errors, connection drops via Toxiproxy or the mesh).

1013
01:02:07.565 --> 01:02:10.551
Load tests beyond capacity to verify shedding.

1014
01:02:10.831 --> 01:02:18.383
Scheduled game days in staging and production with hypotheses, SLO-based success criteria and abort switches.

1015
01:02:19.733 --> 01:02:20.629
Architect.

1016
01:02:20.909 --> 01:02:22.104
Question 15.

1017
01:02:22.384 --> 01:02:26.480
A 25-second database failover caused a 41-minute outage.

1018
01:02:26.760 --> 01:02:28.659
How do you make sure it can't happen again?

1019
01:02:28.939 --> 01:02:30.304
Thirty-second answer:

1020
01:02:30.584 --> 01:02:44.771
Remove the amplifiers: single-layer retries with jitter and budgets, circuit breakers with slow-call detection, bulkheads per dependency, bounded and deadline-aware ingress queues, fallbacks for degradable features, and headroom.

1021
01:02:45.051 --> 01:02:49.509
Add documented load-drop levers and alerts on trigger gone but goodput low.

1022
01:02:49.789 --> 01:02:54.205
Then verify with a game day that repeats the failover under peak-like load.

1023
01:02:55.555 --> 01:02:56.750
Question 16.

1024
01:02:57.030 --> 01:02:59.803
Design admission control for the platform's ingress.

1025
01:03:00.083 --> 01:03:01.449
Thirty-second answer:

1026
01:03:01.729 --> 01:03:14.614
At the gateway: authenticate, rate limit per client and tier (token buckets in Redis), classify requests by criticality (checkout browse prefetch analytics), and shed lower classes first under load.

1027
01:03:14.894 --> 01:03:21.998
At each service: bounded concurrency, deadline-aware dropping, fast 503s with jittered Retry-After.

1028
01:03:22.278 --> 01:03:25.777
Optionally, adaptive concurrency limits driven by latency.

1029
01:03:27.127 --> 01:03:28.471
Question 17.

1030
01:03:28.751 --> 01:03:32.761
When would you use adaptive concurrency limits instead of static bulkheads?

1031
01:03:33.041 --> 01:03:34.407
Thirty-second answer:

1032
01:03:34.687 --> 01:03:44.265
When capacity varies (autoscaling dependencies, noisy neighbours, GC, different request mixes), so static limits are either too tight or too loose.

1033
01:03:44.545 --> 01:03:55.212
Adaptive limits (AIMD, gradient-based, or Netflix concurrency-limits and Envoy adaptive concurrency) adjust the allowed in-flight requests from observed latency.

1034
01:03:56.562 --> 01:03:57.735
Question 18.

1035
01:03:58.015 --> 01:04:01.215
How do resilience patterns interact with a service mesh?

1036
01:04:01.495 --> 01:04:02.861
Thirty-second answer:

1037
01:04:03.141 --> 01:04:09.797
The mesh can do timeouts, retries, outlier detection, connection limits and fault injection at the network level.

1038
01:04:10.077 --> 01:04:15.815
The app does business-aware fallbacks, deadlines, idempotency, and breakers tied to features.

1039
01:04:16.095 --> 01:04:18.079
Assign each concern to one layer.

1040
01:04:18.359 --> 01:04:25.335
Mesh retries plus app retries equals amplification, and mesh timeouts longer than app deadlines are pointless.

1041
01:04:26.685 --> 01:04:27.923
Question 19.

1042
01:04:28.203 --> 01:04:32.085
What is graceful degradation at the business level, and how do you plan it?

1043
01:04:32.365 --> 01:04:33.731
Thirty-second answer:

1044
01:04:34.011 --> 01:04:44.336
Deciding in advance which features can be reduced or turned off under stress, and what users see instead, so critical journeys (browse, checkout, pay, track) survive.

1045
01:04:44.616 --> 01:04:50.632
Plan it with a degradation catalogue: feature, criticality, fallback, owner, flag, test cadence.

1046
01:04:50.912 --> 01:04:51.808
Rehearse it.

1047
01:04:53.158 --> 01:04:54.267
Question 20.

1048
01:04:54.547 --> 01:04:58.217
How do you know your system is resilient, not just configured to be?

1049
01:04:58.497 --> 01:04:59.862
Thirty-second answer:

1050
01:05:00.142 --> 01:05:17.593
Evidence: game days and chaos experiments against SLOs, load tests beyond capacity showing stable goodput, incident metrics (time to recover after the trigger ends), breaker, shed and fallback telemetry during real incidents, and postmortems that close the loop.

1051
01:05:18.943 --> 01:05:19.839
Part 9.

1052
01:05:20.119 --> 01:05:21.527
Summary and Practice.

1053
01:05:22.777 --> 01:05:23.865
Key takeaways.

1054
01:05:24.145 --> 01:05:29.713
Outages are amplification loops, not just failures: retries, queues and waiting callers.

1055
01:05:29.993 --> 01:05:35.305
Timeouts make slow failures fast, and everything else builds on them (Ch 17).

1056
01:05:35.585 --> 01:05:41.451
Retries: one layer, exponential backoff with jitter, bounded, budgeted, idempotent only.

1057
01:05:41.731 --> 01:05:44.441
Without jitter they may never recover (L1).

1058
01:05:44.721 --> 01:05:47.707
Circuit breakers stop the hammering and the waiting (L2:

1059
01:05:47.987 --> 01:05:55.497
296, then 55 calls, p50 802, then 0 ms), and cost some recovery lag.

1060
01:05:55.777 --> 01:05:59.169
Bulkheads per dependency keep failures in their compartment.

1061
01:06:00.519 --> 01:06:06.897
Rate limiting: token bucket for APIs, sliding windows for strict quotas, leaky queues to shape.

1062
01:06:07.177 --> 01:06:10.911
Avoid fixed windows (175 versus 100).

1063
01:06:11.191 --> 01:06:17.271
Global limits need a shared store (240 versus ~133 req per second).

1064
01:06:17.551 --> 01:06:19.215
Shed load early and cheaply.

1065
01:06:19.495 --> 01:06:23.889
Unbounded queues collapse goodput (4 versus 400 req per second).

1066
01:06:24.169 --> 01:06:27.369
Fallbacks must be true, and critical paths fail clearly.

1067
01:06:27.649 --> 01:06:31.105
Keep a degradation catalogue with tested brownout flags.

1068
01:06:31.385 --> 01:06:32.367
Order matters:

1069
01:06:32.647 --> 01:06:36.551
Retry(CircuitBreaker(RateLimiter(TimeLimiter(Bulkhead(call))))).

1070
01:06:37.901 --> 01:06:42.359
Prove it with chaos and load tests, because configuration isn't evidence.

1071
01:06:43.709 --> 01:06:45.032
